# The shared edge proxy: the only thing on the box listening on 80/443.
#
# ## Why this exists as its own project
#
# Caddy used to live *inside* the MiO stack, which made the box single-tenant by
# accident rather than by decision: one container can bind :443, that container
# was built from `frontend/Dockerfile`, and its config was baked into MiO's
# image. Hosting anything else meant editing MiO's Caddyfile and rebuilding
# MiO's web image to deploy an unrelated application.
#
# Now the proxy owns the ports and nothing else, MiO is one upstream behind it,
# and a second project is a second site block plus a shared network. Deploying
# either one no longer restarts the other's front door.
#
# ⚠️ **This file is bind-mounted, not baked.** Adding a site is an edit plus
# `docker compose -p proxy exec caddy caddy reload --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile`,
# with no image rebuild and no downtime for anything already running.

# There is deliberately no global `email` option — see the note in MiO's own
# Caddyfile. An unset variable expands to a bare `email`, which is a parse
# error, and a parse error takes every site on the box down at once. That blast
# radius is larger here than it used to be, which is the cost of sharing.

# --- MiO -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `mio-web` is a network alias on the shared `edge` network, set explicitly in
# `docker-compose.prod.yml` rather than left to Compose's default.
#
# ⛔ The default alias is the *service name*, and service names are only unique
# within a project. Two projects that both call a service `web` would collide on
# a shared network and resolve to whichever container answered first. Every
# upstream added here must carry an explicit, globally unique alias.
#
# TLS is terminated here, so MiO's internal Caddy serves plain HTTP on :80 and
# never sees a certificate. `reverse_proxy` sets `X-Forwarded-Proto` on its own,
# so the upstream can still tell what the browser used.
{$MIO_DOMAIN} {
	# The origin certificate, when there is one.
	#
	# ## Why this is an import and not a `tls` line
	#
	# `mio.dlany.uk` is proxied through Cloudflare (orange cloud) because China
	# blocks the DigitalOcean address, not the hostname — measured 2026-09-07 by
	# a friend in Shanghai, before and after the change. Behind that proxy Caddy
	# cannot renew from Let's Encrypt the way it used to: the default
	# TLS-ALPN-01 challenge needs to terminate TLS, and Cloudflare terminates it
	# first. A certificate that stops renewing takes the whole site down and
	# says nothing until the day it expires.
	#
	# So the droplet serves a **Cloudflare Origin Certificate** instead — valid
	# 15 years, trusted by Cloudflare, and never renewed. It lives in
	# `proxy/certs/`, which is gitignored because it contains a private key.
	#
	# ⛔ **A bare `tls` line here would break CI.** `deploy-stack` boots this
	# same file with `MIO_DOMAIN=localhost` and no certificates on disk, and
	# Caddy refuses to load a `tls` directive whose files are missing. An import
	# glob matching nothing is a *warning* — the same property the sites import
	# below relies on, measured again for this one: an empty directory validates
	# and falls through to automatic HTTPS, which is exactly what CI wants.
	#
	# ⚠️ **An origin certificate is trusted by Cloudflare, not by browsers.** The
	# orange cloud has to stay on. Turning it off serves an untrusted cert to
	# everyone; the way back is to delete `proxy/certs/tls.caddy` and reload,
	# which restores Let's Encrypt.
	import /etc/caddy/certs/*.caddy

	reverse_proxy mio-web:80
}

# --- Other projects --------------------------------------------------------
#
# Drop a `<name>.caddy` file into the sites directory (mounted at
# /etc/caddy/sites) and reload. Nothing in this repo needs to change, which is
# the whole point of the split.
#
#   example.com {
#       reverse_proxy myproject-web:8080
#   }
#
# ⚠️ Matching nothing is a *warning*, not an error — measured, not assumed,
# because an import that failed closed would mean an empty sites directory takes
# every site on the box down. `caddy validate` against an empty directory logs
# `"msg":"No files matching import glob pattern"` and still prints
# `Valid configuration` with exit 0.
import /etc/caddy/sites/*.caddy
